# The plant that pays eighteen percent to put its exchanger thirty-six metres
# from its reactor.
#
# One heat main, eighteen tiles of it, at a percent a tile -- and the throttle
# turned down to 30% so that what arrives is what one exchanger can actually
# boil. Every design in experiment 06 asked what a plant should contain; this
# one asks where it should be, which is a question the drawing could not
# previously tell you the answer to.
#
# It used to be three heat pipes in series at two percent each. The pipes are
# gone and the design is unchanged in every way that matters, which is the
# argument for having deleted them: the connection was always the pipe. What is
# different is that the price is now continuous. Drag the exchanger one tile
# west and the plant gets one percent better, immediately, and you can watch it
# happen -- where before, moving it did nothing at all until it crossed the
# threshold where a whole pipe could be removed.
#
# In experiment 08 it is the design that shows what routing looks like when
# there is somewhere to route to: a lagged main on supports, the length of the
# plot, four metres up.

machine "Long Reach"
brief power

reactor   R1  at 0,0   throttle 30

exchanger HX1 at 22,0
turbine   T1  at 26,0
generator G1  at 30,0

pump      W1  at 22,4

wire R1.heat -> HX1.heat

wire W1.water -> HX1.water
wire HX1.steam -> T1.steam
wire T1.rotary -> G1.rotary
